OpenPIA — Licensing
===================

OpenPIA is licensed for maximum adoption with minimum friction. Different
artifact types carry different licences; project *coherence* is carried
separately, by trademark — not by the copyright licence.

What applies to what
--------------------

  Schema files & data model (schema/)             Apache License 2.0
  Reference implementations & tooling             Apache License 2.0
  Specification & documentation (spec/, docs/)    CC BY 4.0
  Name "OpenPIA", logo, "OpenPIA-conformant"      Trademark + conformance
                                                  policy (not a copyright licence)

Apache-2.0 covers the schema and code because it is permissive and includes an
explicit, royalty-free *patent grant* — important protection for an interchange
standard. CC BY 4.0 covers the prose so it can be reproduced, translated and
quoted freely, with attribution. You may use, copy, implement and build on all
of it, including in closed-source and commercial products, at no cost.

Full licence texts:
  Apache-2.0   see LICENSE-APACHE-2.0   (canonical: https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0)
  CC BY 4.0    see LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0    (canonical: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode)

Coherence — the name, not the licence
-------------------------------------

The schema and docs are free to fork, copy, translate and extend. What is
protected is the *name*: "OpenPIA", the logo, and the claim "OpenPIA-conformant".
A fork may do anything it likes with the schema, but may not present itself as
OpenPIA, or claim OpenPIA conformance, unless it conforms to the canonical
version. This keeps adoption frictionless while ensuring "OpenPIA" continues to
mean one specific, interoperable thing. See CHARTER.md and GOVERNANCE.md.

Copyright
---------

Copyright (c) the OpenPIA contributors.
"OpenPIA" is a trademark of the OpenPIA project, intended to be held in trust
for the future industry steward.

